> I suggest that, if a cloudy potion or magic lamp is cursed, there
> should be a chance that the angry djinni from it will pretend to act
> like a wish-granting one, up until it actually becomes wish-granting
> time.  Go ahead, wish for a blessed fixed wand of death, and the djinni
> will make it appear - unfortunately, you failed to specify that said
> wand should be in your hands, not those of that demon it just spawned a
> few spaces away.  Want some gray dragon scale mail?  The djinni will be
> more than happy to provide you with everything you need to make your
> own - a blessed scroll of enchant armor and a colossal horde of gray
> dragons.

It's a really cool idea, and makes sense -- whoever heard of a djinni
attacking someone who rubbed his lamp with his bare hands?  The problem
I can see is that there are a huge number of potential things to wish
for, each one potentially requiring its own special case in the code to
handle.

The idea is still not without merit however.  My suggestion is to code
up a good number of those special cases (probably in a switch
statement), but have a fallback, generic result for those cases for
which ingenuity fails.  I'd imagine the basic cases are

Equipment: Give the player the item but make it cursed and of minus to
any requested plus, or put the item on a humanoid monster (player
monsters preferably) that can use it and send him in.  That also works
for monster-usable wands.

Rings: Monsters don't use rings, but they should be cursed in any case.
 Resistance-granting rings could be generated in the inventory of a
monster strong against a player without that resistance.

Fallthrough case: Curse the item.

Gold: Teleporting to Ludios is too much (and possibly useful, and
potentially presents technical issues).  Teleporting him into a vault
if one's on the level seems like a good idea, but considering that a
wish for gold is one of the more useless things a player could ask for,
the djinni might just figure the player's lack of imagination is
punishment enough and grant his request.

